WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.953
JENNY BROWN: File storage
is a critical need

00:00:01.953 --> 00:00:04.950
for many enterprise
applications and workloads.

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:07.860
But file storage is about
more than just disk space.

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:09.480
It's about finding
a solution that

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:13.800
is fast, consistent, scalable,
and still simple to use.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:16.686
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:23.400
Some of the most
common use cases

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:26.260
for file storage in
enterprise applications

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:28.740
are media rendering,
data analytics,

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:30.810
and managing shared content.

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:34.230
These complex situations
require flexible and scalable

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:36.450
solutions.

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:38.340
Plenty of us have
heard it before.

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:41.670
Storage is cheap, but with
needs growing exponentially

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:45.360
to fit use cases like these,
companies want flexibility

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:48.720
to scale on demand and
meet specific performance

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:51.990
needs of their application
and user demands.

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:53.490
And now that
companies are turning

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:56.250
to cloud computing
solutions for applications

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:58.110
and for their
infrastructure, there's

00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:01.530
a growing demand for
cloud-based file systems.

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:03.330
To meet the needs
of customers looking

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:06.540
for a fully managed
cloud-based storage product,

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:09.730
Google offers Filestore.

00:01:09.730 --> 00:01:12.790
Filestore gives users a
simple native experience

00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:16.060
for standing up managed Network
Attached Storage, or NAS,

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:18.160
for their compute engine
and Google Kubernetes

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:19.780
engine instances.

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:22.990
We also rely on file storage
partners such as NetApp

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:26.350
and Dell EMC Isilon
Today, we're going

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:28.960
to show you how to quickly
configure and attach your file

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:32.230
system to a compute instance.

00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:35.350
Say we have an intensive
workload and many clients,

00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:37.780
and high throughput is critical.

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:43.940
To begin, I'll name my instance,
for example, Performance Test.

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:46.970
Next, I have to
select a service tier.

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:49.100
Since I need a
high-performance application

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:53.090
with many connected clients,
I'll go with High Scale.

00:01:53.090 --> 00:01:55.380
For help picking the tier
that's right for you,

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:59.570
you can check out
the comparison table.

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:03.810
The storage type for High Scale
is SSD, which works for me,

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:07.950
and with Basic, I would
also have the option of HDD.

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:10.139
I'll keep capacity
at 60 terabytes

00:02:10.139 --> 00:02:13.500
and can always scale up or down
when needed without any service

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:15.220
interruption.

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:17.630
As I specify
different capacities,

00:02:17.630 --> 00:02:20.920
I can check the calculator for
cost and performance estimates.

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:27.260
Now I have to choose a location.

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:30.050
Since my clients are
located in US Central 1,

00:02:30.050 --> 00:02:32.570
the default location is perfect.

00:02:32.570 --> 00:02:37.220
I can set a specific zone or let
the deployment decide for me.

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:40.010
Next, I'll select the
network used by my clients

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:43.260
so they'll be able to
access this file system.

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:46.100
Once I have a network,
I'll allocate an IP range

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:48.260
or let the service
do this for me.

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:50.870
The file system mount
point IP address will

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:52.235
be selected from this range.

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:00.810
Next, I'll give my
share a name, DCC.

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:04.050
This will be represented in
the mount location later.

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:05.940
Even though I can
grant or restrict

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:09.510
access to my file system
through IP-based controls,

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:11.430
I'll leave it open for now.

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:15.360
And now I'm ready to
create my instance.

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:18.150
Once it's ready, I can see
all my instance details

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:19.755
and copy the mount point string.

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:26.890
Now I'll SSH into a client
VM I already created

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:29.560
and mount my file system
using the mount point I copied

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:31.780
earlier to slash MNT directory.

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:39.290
Let's run some load and take
a look at the performance.

00:03:39.290 --> 00:03:42.770
I'm starting with 256 clients
performing simultaneously

00:03:42.770 --> 00:03:44.570
with intensive IOPs.

00:03:44.570 --> 00:03:48.280
You can see the clients are
building their working set.

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:50.470
And here you can see
the latency leveling off

00:03:50.470 --> 00:03:53.920
at 10 milliseconds and
IOPs reaching 150,000,

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:57.350
since my clients are
saturating the instance.

00:03:57.350 --> 00:04:00.040
I need higher performance
and more capacity,

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:02.260
so I'm going to scale
up my instance from 60

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:04.900
terabytes to 320 terabytes.

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:07.780
After my instance scales up,
you can see the graphs start

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:09.280
to change.

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:13.720
Here the latency graph drops,
showing much lower latency,

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:17.440
and the IOPs graph is
climbing to 600,000 IOPs.

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:19.329
As we'd expect, the
total throughput

00:04:19.329 --> 00:04:23.190
is also growing accordingly.

00:04:23.190 --> 00:04:25.740
For anyone that's ever
managed their own storage

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:28.860
and panicked because they needed
to slap another physical drive

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:31.020
in a server before
they ran out of space,

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:33.400
this is pretty exciting.

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:35.470
Today, we saw how
Google Cloud file

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:38.620
storage is used to migrate
traditional applications,

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:41.680
modernize existing
applications with Kubernetes,

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:43.510
and scale to meet the
performance demands

00:04:43.510 --> 00:04:45.340
of big compute workloads.

00:04:45.340 --> 00:04:48.160
For media rendering to
genomics processing,

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:50.050
Google's file storage
can meet the needs

00:04:50.050 --> 00:04:52.360
of business critical workloads.

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:54.220
Thanks for joining us,
and please check out

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:56.110
our website and
documentation to learn

00:04:56.110 --> 00:04:58.000
even more about Filestore.

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:01.050
[MUSIC PLAYING]

